Ask the frontend for the path, not the bytes

retro_get_system_info leaves need_fullpath false everywhere but GEKKO, so a
frontend reads the whole ROM and hands over a pointer -- and retro_load_game
then takes a second copy of it:

    data = anonymousMemoryMap(game->size);
    memcpy(data, game->data, game->size);
    rom = VFileFromMemory(data, game->size);

Two copies of a 32 MB cart on a handheld with 101 MB of RAM and no swap. The
branch right below already does the better thing where ENABLE_VFS is defined,
which it is for the libretro core (CMakeLists sets it in COMPILE_DEFINITIONS):
VFileOpen on the path, with the kernel paging the file in and caching it like
any other mapped file.

Measured on a Miyoo Mini Flip, where slot holds about 44 MB of its own before
a core is loaded: Mother 3 could not be loaded on mGBA at all, and Kirby's
8 MB cart cost 16 MB across the two copies.

Keyed on ENABLE_VFS rather than on the platform, since that is exactly the
condition under which retro_load_game can open a path at all.

Measured on the handheld with both halves in, Kirby & the Amazing Mirror on
mGBA -- the cart that cost 16 MB across the two copies:

    VmRSS 64.4 MB   anon 25.2 MB   MemAvailable 38.1 MB
    16384 kB  /mnt/SDCARD/Games/GBA/Kirby & the Amazing Mirror (USA).gba

The ROM is a file mapping now rather than anonymous memory, so the kernel
pages it in on demand and can evict it under pressure -- which is the part
that matters on a device with no swap. The same cart on gpSP, before any of
this, had 1.9 MB free and was being killed.

Paired with the frontend honouring need_fullpath (patches/0019 in this port).
Without that, this removes mGBA's own copy and the frontend still reads the
file into memory for nothing.

diff --git a/src/platform/libretro/libretro.c b/src/platform/libretro/libretro.c
index d4de13f..ae85234 100644
--- a/src/platform/libretro/libretro.c
+++ b/src/platform/libretro/libretro.c
@@ -1327,7 +1405,13 @@ void retro_set_input_state(retro_input_state_t input) {
 }
 
 void retro_get_system_info(struct retro_system_info* info) {
-#ifdef GEKKO
+#ifdef ENABLE_VFS
+	/* Ask for the path, not the bytes: retro_load_game opens it with VFileOpen
+	 * below, where the kernel pages the file in and caches it. Saying false
+	 * here has the frontend read the whole ROM and hand over a pointer, and
+	 * then this core takes a second copy of it with anonymousMemoryMap and
+	 * memcpy -- two copies of a 32 MB cart on a 101 MB handheld with no swap,
+	 * which is more than it has to spare. */
 	info->need_fullpath = true;
 #else
 	info->need_fullpath = false;
